 ],
 "literal": "Compassion and love having discarded, companion having abandoned, / for the sake of wealth those who separate, strong ones if they are, / let the strong ones be the strong ones; / let us be simple, we women indeed!",
 "translation": "If those who abandon their companion, casting aside grace and love, to depart in search of wealth are considered strong-willed, then let those strong-willed men remain strong-willed. As for us, simple women, let us remain simple.",
 "modern": "அருளையும் அன்பையும் நீக்கிவிட்டு, தன் துணையைத் துறந்து, பொருளின் பொருட்டுப் பிரிந்து செல்பவர்கள் வலிமை உடையவர்கள் என்றால், அந்த வலிமை உடையவர்கள் வலிமை உடையவர்களாகவே இருக்கட்டும். பெண்ணாகிய நாம் அறியாமை உடையவர்களாகவே இருப்போம்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her confidante when the hero has left her to acquire wealth. It belongs to the Pālai tiṇai, which depicts the painful separation of lovers. The heroine uses gentle irony to cope with her grief, contrasting the 'strength' of the hero with her own 'simplicity'. If being strong-willed (uravōr) means having the coldness to abandon love and a partner for material gain, she declares that she has no desire for such strength. Instead, she and her companion are content to remain 'simple' or 'foolish' (maṭavam) women who value love above all else.",
